Sarah should be treated as a **genuinely non-technical customer**.

Do not assume that she understands phrases simply because they may be familiar to people working with cars.

For example:

### “Diagnostics”

Sarah might interpret this simply as:

> “They plug the car into a computer and it tells them what's wrong.”

The website should help her understand if the real process is more involved than that.

### “Fault code”

She may assume:

> “The computer tells them which part needs replacing.”

If Okee has a more sophisticated diagnostic approach, this needs to be explained simply.

### “Auto electrics”

Sarah may not know whether a dashboard warning light means she needs this service.

### “ADAS”

Sarah is very unlikely to know what this means.

### “Calibration”

She is unlikely to understand why this might ever be required.

The audit should therefore judge technical terminology according to a simple test:

> **Can Sarah understand enough to make the next decision without needing to become knowledgeable about cars first?**

