## CUSTOMER LANGUAGE

Sarah is much more likely to say:

> “A yellow light came on.”

than:

> “I have an engine-management fault.”

She is more likely to say:

> “It doesn't seem to start quite right.”

than:

> “I have an intermittent starting issue.”

She is more likely to say:

> “It sort of hesitates when I pull away.”

than:

> “There is a hesitation under acceleration.”

She is more likely to say:

> “The car sounds different.”

than:

> “There is an abnormal mechanical noise.”

The audit should specifically check whether Okee bridges the gap between **customer language** and **garage terminology**.

---

## CLAIM VS PROOF

Sarah is unlikely to understand the technical importance of every qualification.

Therefore, do not simply ask:

> “Does Okee display qualifications?”

Also ask:

> **“Does Sarah understand why those qualifications should make her feel safer choosing Okee?”**

For example:

### CLAIM

“We are diagnostic specialists.”

### TECHNICAL PROOF

Bosch qualifications, diagnostic equipment, ATA credentials and specialist training.

### CUSTOMER TRANSLATION

> “We don't simply read a fault code and start replacing parts. Our trained technicians investigate what is actually causing the problem.”

The strongest website communicates **all three layers**.

---

## EMOTIONAL TRUST

For this persona, emotional reassurance matters more than it does for a more technically confident customer.

Pay particular attention to whether Okee feels:

* Approachable
* Patient
* Friendly
* Non-judgemental
* Transparent
* Comfortable dealing with people who know nothing about cars

Look for evidence rather than assuming this from tone alone.

Relevant proof could include:

* Customer reviews
* Team profiles
* Explained processes
* Real photography
* Guarantees
* Clear authorisation procedures
* Customer-focused language
* Facilities
* Callback or advice options

---

## CONVERSION TEST

Sarah should ideally be able to move through a journey resembling:

> **“Something doesn't feel right.”**

↓

> **“I don't need to know what's wrong.”**

↓

> **“Okee can investigate it properly.”**

↓

> **“They'll explain what they find.”**

↓

> **“They won't simply do expensive work without discussing it with me.”**

↓

> **“Other normal customers trust them.”**

↓

> **“I know how to get help.”**

↓

> **Contact Okee**

If the website forces Sarah to understand technical services before she can comfortably take action, identify this as significant friction.

